=====Usage notes=====
Like other Norwegian [[holiday]] names, {{m|nb|jul}} normally has a lowercase initial.

Both {{m|nb|julen}} (masculine definite singular) and {{m|nb|jula}} (feminine definite singular) are standard. The bare form occurs in expressions such as ''til jul'' (“for [[Christmas]]”) and ''feire jul'' (“celebrate Christmas”); ''i jula'' and ''i julen'' mean “during/over Christmas”.

In Norway, the main family celebration takes place on [[Christmas Eve]], the evening of 24 December. {{m|nb|juledag|Første juledag}} is 25 December, and {{m|nb|juledag|andre juledag}} is 26 December. The period called ''jul'' varies with context; traditional endpoints include 6 January and 13 January.

Compounds usually have the first-element form ''jule-'', as in {{m|nb|juletre|t=Christmas tree}}.

Common expressions include:
* ''feire jul'', ''holde jul'', ''ture jul'': celebrate Christmas.
* {{m|nb|god jul}} or ''gledelig jul'': [[Merry Christmas]].
* ''hvit jul'': a [[white Christmas]], with snow; also used in sobriety campaigns for an [[alcohol-free]] Christmas.
* ''svart jul'' or ''grønn jul'': a Christmas without [[snow]].
* ''ringe julen inn'' or ''synge julen inn'': welcome Christmas with bells or singing.
* ''drikke jul'': historically, celebrate Christmas or [[Yule]] by drinking, especially [[ale]]; figuratively, have a merry time.
* ''bære jula ut'' or ''bære julen ut'': traditionally, leave a Christmas visit without having been offered food or drink; also, bring the Christmas festivities to an end.
* ''det er jul bare en gang i året'' (also ''… om året''): [[Christmas comes but once a year]], used to justify enjoying oneself or indulging a little.

